WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.892 --> 00:00:09.329
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.064 --> 00:00:30.230
MALE SPEAKER: Ladies and
gentlemen, take your seats.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:31.842
Our program is beginning now.

00:00:45.312 --> 00:00:46.270
DAN BELCHER: All right.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.870
Good morning, everyone.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:49.950
My name's Dan Belcher.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.160
I'm joined by my colleague
Vas Bala and David Hayes

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.030
from PagerDuty.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.730
I hope you enjoy the keynote.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.120
It's a good session.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.710
All right.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.340
At the keynote, we announced
Google Stackdriver.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.410
And that's what this
session is all about.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:10.700
It's about managing workloads
across cloud providers.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.200
Since this was the
first day that we

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.480
announced Google Stackdriver, we
have a lot of ground to cover.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.110
And so what we'll
do is first I'll

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:20.220
provide an overview of
the Stackdriver suite.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.610
We'll do a demo with
a particular focus

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.860
on the monitoring and
logging components.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.980
There will be a session
this afternoon focused

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.710
on the diagnostics components.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.140
And that's Stackdriver
trace, Stackdriver debug,

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:36.994
and Stackdriver error reporting,
which we announced yesterday.

00:01:36.994 --> 00:01:38.410
Then I'll hand it
off to David who

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:42.750
will talk about how Stackdriver
and PagerDuty work together.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.970
And then finally, Vas will
talk about Stackdriver

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.300
in the broader context
of Google Cloud Platform

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:55.110
and how you can use tools like
BigQuery and Cloud Datalab

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:59.440
to extend the value of
your Stackdriver data.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.510
So for context,
one of the things

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:05.370
that we believe at
Stackdriver is that visibility

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.480
shouldn't be so painful.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:12.560
We started this journey
focused on monitoring in 2012.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.310
And at the time, we met
with many, many customers

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.547
who were running applications
at scale in the cloud.

00:02:19.547 --> 00:02:21.130
And broadly I could
say that they fell

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.590
into one of two categories.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.410
The first category,
they'd say well,

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.000
we really don't
have the visibility

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.520
that we needed to
our application.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.620
We're relying on some basic
capabilities from the Cloud

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.700
Platform, but frequently we're
surprised by performance issues

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.410
or outages or capacity issues.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.490
So that was one
category of customer.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.810
The second category
of customer would say,

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.430
actually then we're all set.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.030
We have great visibility
into our systems.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.790
We have actually
two teams that built

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:55.240
and maintain monitoring and
logging systems on our payroll.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.070
And those are
teams actually that

00:02:58.070 --> 00:02:59.490
are doing that
instead of helping

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.110
to build our applications.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:05.060
And so one thing at
Google Stackdriver

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.474
is that visibility
shouldn't be so painful.

00:03:07.474 --> 00:03:10.140
Another thing that we believe is
that we should be providing you

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:16.230
with insights to do your job,
not just lots and lots of data.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.260
Because data actually
isn't that hard to come

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.650
by in monitoring and logging.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:23.450
There are many ways that you
can get metrics, many ways

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:26.870
to get traces, logs, lots
of tools available for data

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.900
visualization, log analysis,
uptime monitoring, application

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.440
performance management, right?

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.240
There's a lot signals that you
can get related to performance.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.220
In fact, alerting is
just another signal.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.000
How many people have gotten
more than 10 alerts today

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.290
for an application?

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.910
100 today for an application?

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.802
A few, a few.

00:03:50.802 --> 00:03:53.260
And when we talk about providing
insight, what we're really

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:58.380
thinking about is that silos of
data don't really help, right?

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.190
And this is one actually that
is important to us at Google.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:05.190
And it's really difficult to
organize this type of data

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:08.740
and make it useful for people
who are operating applications

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.410
at scale if the data
is stuck in silos.

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:15.630
So part of Google Stackdriver
is about bringing that data

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:19.370
together so that we can
make it more useful.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:21.120
Finally, this may be
a controversial thing

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.009
to say at the Google
Cloud Conference,

00:04:24.009 --> 00:04:25.050
but, I'm going to say it.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.460
This isn't about Google Cloud.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.670
It's not about AWS.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.480
In fact, I'm also
sort of in love

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.926
with metrics and logs and
alerts and that kind of thing.

00:04:34.926 --> 00:04:36.050
But it's not about metrics.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:36.883
It's not about logs.

00:04:36.883 --> 00:04:37.900
It's not about alerts.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.420
It's about you.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:41.950
It's about the people who
are responsible for keeping

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:44.810
applications fast and
available and providing you

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:49.130
with the tools that you
need to do your job.

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:52.920
And the sort of general
theme that we're focused on

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:57.250
with Google Stackdriver is
making ops as easy as possible.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:00.750
And our definition of easy is
can it be really easy for you

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.630
to find problems wherever
they're occurring

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.027
in your application stack?

00:05:05.027 --> 00:05:07.110
Can we make it easy for
you to fix those problems,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.130
get to root cause.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:12.190
Can we help you answer
the very basic questions

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:13.950
that you need to
ask of your data,

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.790
like do I have enough capacity?

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:19.310
Are my users running
into errors when

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:21.820
they're using my application?

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.860
And can we do all that without
creating a really complicated

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.680
system that you
need to integrate

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.280
and manage and maintain?

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.570
And so that's what
Stackdriver is all about.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:37.230
At a high level, as we announce
Google Stackdriver today,

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.390
there are five components.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.992
Two, we did a brief demo
on stage in the keynote,

00:05:41.992 --> 00:05:43.450
and we'll go deeper
into those two.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:44.610
That's monitoring.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:48.290
Think of that as alerts,
metrics, logs, and so forth.

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:50.690
Logging, which is
exactly as it sounds,

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:53.220
a really scalable
logging pipeline

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.470
that can accept system logs,
application logs, requests logs

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.970
and so forth and make those
searchable and available

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:00.120
to you.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.530
And then three diagnostic tools.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:04.980
One debug, so you
can debug issues

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.010
with your code in production.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:10.550
Cloud trace, distributed tracing
for App Engine applications

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:14.930
so you can compare latency for
example between two releases.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.530
And then error reporting,
a really exciting tool

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:20.620
that we announced just yesterday
that will automatically

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:24.480
detect when there are a lot of
one new type of error occurring

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.990
in your application and
notify you when that happens.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:29.620
So this is the
Stackdriver suite.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.570
We'll go deeper into
monitoring and logging here.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:37.500
Just for context, the way that
we think about integrating,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.240
so we think about
your application

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.480
stack-- so integrating at
the Cloud Platform layer,

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:46.440
integrating at the system layer,
integrating at the application

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.990
layer, and collecting
lots of signals,

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:53.700
whether that's logs or
metrics or traces or errors,

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.560
and then exposing
features that make

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.644
that data available to you.

00:06:57.644 --> 00:06:59.310
So we'll jump into
the demo and show you

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:00.482
how this all comes together.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.550
So this is the
Stackdriver home page.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.050
You may have noticed
in the keynote,

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.310
we started with
a dark them, then

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:09.434
we jumped to a light theme.

00:07:09.434 --> 00:07:12.900
That's one of the advantages
of the monitoring interface.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.280
You can choose whether you use
a dark theme or a light theme.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.490
I'll give you a quick tour
of the primary console.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:23.300
Here in the top left
you see uptime checks.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.740
So this is basically
probes that you

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:30.490
can configure to tell
Google servers to connect

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:34.030
to your internet
accessible endpoints

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:37.480
periodically and confirm that
they're up and available.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:41.380
So this can connect over
HTTPS or HTTP or UDP.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.070
Think of it as a
way of confirming

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:45.684
that your end users can access
your APIs and load balancers

00:07:45.684 --> 00:07:46.850
and front ends and so forth.

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:53.590
Next below that, we
actually have a preview

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:56.135
of a new feature that's
related to our integration

00:07:56.135 --> 00:07:56.760
with PagerDuty.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.660
You can see that,
thank god, David is

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:03.702
on call right now in PagerDuty.

00:08:03.702 --> 00:08:05.160
And if I wanted to
expand that, you

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.040
could see actually
who's on call next

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:09.570
and who's escalation
and so forth.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:12.160
Below that you see sort
of at a glance help.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.640
So this is where we try
to roll up these signals

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:17.572
into a very simple
way of knowing

00:08:17.572 --> 00:08:18.780
whether there are any issues.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.120
So here I see I have a
few App Engine modules .

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:22.961
And they're green.

00:08:22.961 --> 00:08:24.960
We know there aren't any
alerts related to them.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:26.790
I don't have to
worry about them.

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.050
Likewise there's a notion
of groups and applications.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:34.230
So this is a really fundamental
concept in Stackdriver

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:39.530
where you describe how you think
about your cloud environment.

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:43.010
So you can define groups
based on, for example,

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:44.059
cloud accounts.

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:48.220
So if you have GCP
projects or AWS accounts,

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:51.020
you could group those
together in your Stackdriver

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:52.180
application.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:54.590
If you have microservices,
you can define

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.040
groups for your microservices.

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.750
Now the important
thing with these groups

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:00.980
is you can define them
based on, for example,

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:04.390
naming conventions or tags.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:07.640
And here I could choose
an AWS Auto Scaling group.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:11.390
And I'd have a Stackdriver
group that corresponds to that.

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:14.340
So as new instances
are added to the group,

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.025
those instances are brought
together in Stackdriver.

00:09:17.025 --> 00:09:18.400
And that becomes
really important

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:22.530
when you want to have dashboards
and alerting policies that

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:24.460
represent sort of
the architecture

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:26.557
of your application in
the way that you think.

00:09:26.557 --> 00:09:28.640
And we'll explore that in
a little bit more detail

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:30.440
in a moment.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:34.490
And then in the top right hand
corner of the Stackdriver, home

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.079
page you see incidents.

00:09:36.079 --> 00:09:37.620
So this is where
you would see if you

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.870
have any alerting policies
that have triggered.

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:43.830
And we'll show you how to
configure alerts in a moment.

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:45.380
And then finally
on the home page

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.930
you have your sort
of, at a glance view

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:50.680
into the health of your
environment in terms

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.570
of key performance indicators.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:54.940
So here the
application that I, I

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:59.930
have an Elastic Load
Balancer sending requests.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:01.730
That's being routed
through an nGenx proxy.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:03.104
And then traffic's
being balanced

00:10:03.104 --> 00:10:06.960
across clusters on GCP and AWS.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:08.530
And so these might
be the view that I

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:11.890
want, the metrics that I want
to be looking at every time

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:12.970
I open the application.

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:15.610
This is very configurable.

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:18.210
And then below I see
recent events or changes

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:19.070
to my application.

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:20.940
So this is where
you can see that I

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:22.710
have some alerts in
the demo environment

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.460
that triggered this morning.

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:26.480
Here is also where
I would see when

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:31.230
people make changes, bring new
instances online, and so forth.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:36.125
So that's sort of the overview
of the Stackdriver home page.

00:10:36.125 --> 00:10:38.250
Now I want to just give
you a little bit of context

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.060
in terms of how you get set up.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:42.820
The first step in
setting up Stackdriver

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:49.170
would be to basically associate
your GCP projects and AWS

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:52.660
accounts to a
Stackdriver environment.

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:56.680
So here you see that I have six
or seven Google Cloud Platform

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:57.600
projects.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:01.149
If I want to add a new project,
all I have to do is click Add.

00:11:01.149 --> 00:11:02.690
I can select the
projects that I want

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:07.470
to add, and click Add Projects.

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:11.590
Connecting an AWS account
instead of being one step

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:12.650
is just two steps.

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:14.270
So the first thing
that you would do

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:17.850
is create a cross account
identity and access management

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:20.900
role inside of your
Amazon console that

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:23.970
grants Stackdriver access
to query the Amazon

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:26.620
APIs on your behalf.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:28.980
And once that identity
and access management

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.340
role has been
created, then all you

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:35.380
do is paste the Amazon
resource name into this field

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:39.310
and click Submit, and
you'll be up and running.

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:41.425
Now when I say up and
running, what that means

00:11:41.425 --> 00:11:43.550
is you'll have a bunch of
dashboards and visibility

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:46.560
by default. So you don't have
to go and set anything else up.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:48.850
In about two minutes
you'll see, for example,

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:50.980
an inventory of
all your instances,

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:56.020
whether they're running on
Google or Amazon, in one list.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.560
And this list could contain--
here I only have 116 instances.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:04.470
It could be 1,016 or 10,016,
it doesn't really matter.

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:07.120
And I could easily search and
filter across those instances.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:12.320
So if I want to look at my
Apache nodes, I can do that.

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:13.540
That's one default view.

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:15.310
Another one might
be if I'm using,

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:20.350
as I am in this case,
Apache web server.

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:24.280
So here, the Apache
web server metrics

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:27.740
are actually being collected
by our monitoring agent.

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:31.520
Our monitoring agent is
a package of collectd.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:33.510
collectd is an open
source monitoring

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.930
daemon with a rich
community, broad deployment.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:38.450
I bet many of you
are already using it.

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:40.750
And collectd also has
plugins to monitor things

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:45.940
like Apache, nGenx,
RabbitMQ, MongoDB, Elastic

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:48.320
Search, Cassandra, you
can go down the list.

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:50.540
And Stackdriver really
thinks that these

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:55.972
are sort of native services
that many of our customers

00:12:55.972 --> 00:12:56.680
will want to use.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:59.080
And so what we've done
is created dashboards

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:01.170
that automatically
show the relevant data

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:05.297
from those services once you've
deployed the monitoring agent.

00:13:05.297 --> 00:13:06.630
And you can go deeper from here.

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:09.990
So I can look at, here
I have Apache metrics.

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:12.420
I could look at
host level metrics,

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:15.937
zoom in to a particular
time period, and so forth.

00:13:15.937 --> 00:13:17.770
You'll see a similar
view on the cloud side.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:22.920
So for example, when I
configure that AWS connection,

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.450
I could see my
Elastic Load Balancers

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:27.600
on preconfigured dashboards.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.800
They're very rich
and designed to help

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:32.980
support the workflows that
you use when you have issues.

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:36.180
So for example, here these
are all of my Elastic Load

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:37.900
Balancers.

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:40.510
Here I want to look at one.

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:43.610
And when I drill to look at
that Elastic Load Balancer,

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:47.710
it's fronting a set of nodes.

00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:52.910
And so I can quickly gather
key metrics for the ELB,

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:56.280
like latency, like requests,
like response codes,

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:57.950
and so forth.

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:00.090
But when I have an
issue, for example

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.400
that users are
getting errors, I may

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:05.390
want to try to isolate that
to an individual availability

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:06.110
zone.

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:10.540
So I can drill in and look at
latency, requests, response

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:14.320
codes by availability zone.

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:21.800
So here I can see, for example,
400 errors in us-west-2b.

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:25.240
And they're higher than
the others in the cluster.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:27.290
Another case I could
actually go behind the load

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:30.250
balancer and look at
the individual posts.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:33.010
So I can have a cluster
of hundreds of machines.

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.390
And this would give me a
way to drill in and look

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:39.970
at the capacity utilization of
the machines in the cluster.

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:41.600
So that's sort of
the native experience

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:43.725
that you would get with a
service like Elastic Load

00:14:43.725 --> 00:14:44.510
Balancer.

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:46.380
And you'd see a
very similar model

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:47.990
whether you use on
AWS, whether you're

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:51.710
using ELB or RDS or
DynamoDB or Kinesis,

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:54.630
and you could go down
the list of integrations

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.510
that are available.

00:14:57.510 --> 00:14:59.260
All right, I guess
it's about time that we

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:00.260
talk about Google Cloud.

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:04.590
So here you'll see, for example,
a dashboard for Pub/Sub.

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:06.670
In this environment
I'm using Pub/Sub.

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:09.470
And again, out of the box,
we get rich dashboards

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:13.340
for Pub/Sub, for App
Engine, for Cloud SQL.

00:15:13.340 --> 00:15:15.760
You can go down the
list of GCP services

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:17.710
where I would have a
very similar experience.

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:23.280
And so this is sort of
how Stackdriver integrates

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:25.811
at that Cloud Platform
layer, at that system layer,

00:15:25.811 --> 00:15:27.727
and some of these
application building blocks.

00:15:30.519 --> 00:15:32.310
But these are all
preconfigured dashboards.

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:34.570
I don't know about
you, but I like

00:15:34.570 --> 00:15:37.010
to create my own dashboards
and put them in the format

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:38.130
that I want.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:42.460
And so here I can jump
in to look at a dashboard

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:44.416
that I've configured.

00:15:44.416 --> 00:15:46.290
And it sort of brings
together these metrics,

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:49.500
whether they're on AWS
or GCP, whether they're

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:53.980
at the system level or the open
source sort of building blocks.

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:55.870
And here I might
want to actually look

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:58.740
at a view that shows a
little bit more data.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:03.600
And maybe I want to
add more metrics.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:05.860
And a couple really
important dimensions to this

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:09.130
are, we don't stop at
that open source layer.

00:16:09.130 --> 00:16:11.750
We can go higher into
the application stack.

00:16:11.750 --> 00:16:15.310
So you can send custom
metrics that you

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:18.290
define that might be the
starting point for looking

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:20.020
at the health of
your environment.

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:28.200
So in my case, I have a custom
editor for job completion time.

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:31.090
Now this is strictly
from my application.

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:34.300
There isn't an agent that
understands it natively.

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:37.200
And that's available
for me to chart,

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:38.930
to create alerts
on, and so forth.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:41.190
And so I can add
it to my dashboard.

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:48.410
Finally, we can also get
visibility from our log data.

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:52.110
So we'll jump to the
logs in a moment.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:54.330
But we can look at log metrics.

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:57.800
So these are metrics that I
define based on my log data.

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:00.350
So for example,
if I specifically

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:05.599
wanted to look at 404
errors in my Apache logs,

00:17:05.599 --> 00:17:08.560
I could create a metric that
has those flow into the system

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:11.569
and bring that together
with the other data.

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:16.730
So here I'll save that metric.

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:19.609
And so one of the great things
that we talked about earlier

00:17:19.609 --> 00:17:21.240
is how this data
all comes together.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:24.240
And we try to make it as
insightful as possible.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:27.619
Here I'll preview a
couple new features

00:17:27.619 --> 00:17:29.070
that you'll have access to soon.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:30.810
They're available on alpha.

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:36.490
The first is giving you insight
into kind of core statistics

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:38.550
across all of my charts.

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:41.940
So for example, if I go
to the three column view,

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:45.390
I can see at a glance what the
maximum and minimum values are

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:47.280
for a given time frame.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:55.780
I can also expand this to
look at all of the legends

00:17:55.780 --> 00:18:00.260
where I could, for example,
chart the standard deviation

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:03.530
across a large set
of data, or the mean

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:05.300
across a large set of data.

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:08.620
So if you're managing hundreds
or thousands of machines,

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:10.360
sometimes you don't
want to have a yarn

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:12.540
ball of lines on your chart.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.730
You want to look at
your kind of key metrics

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:16.034
across these large clusters.

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:23.710
Secondly, in terms of
providing insights,

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:25.630
one of the things again
that we're working on

00:18:25.630 --> 00:18:27.350
that I want to give
you a preview of

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:30.390
is the notion of vital signs.

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:31.930
So as I talked
about earlier where

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:34.400
we have all these
different ways of checking

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:37.000
to see if something's healthy,
what our customers have been

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:40.130
asking us for is kind
of a scorecard view

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:42.740
into the health of
their applications.

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:44.480
And so what vital
signs is all about

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:47.340
is when you describe
how you think

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.720
about your application using the
group concept that we discussed

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:54.680
before, we'll be able to
present a standard set of sort

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:57.274
of dimensions of health
of your application

00:18:57.274 --> 00:18:58.940
and whether your
application is actually

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:00.231
healthy along those dimensions.

00:19:00.231 --> 00:19:02.520
We're really excited
about this innovation

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:04.610
and look forward to
making it available soon.

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:11.219
So that's most of the sort of
monitoring side of the system.

00:19:11.219 --> 00:19:12.760
But when we talked
earlier, we talked

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:15.720
about how we want to support
really rich workflows.

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:18.130
And sometimes it's not
just about the alerts

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:19.342
and the metrics.

00:19:19.342 --> 00:19:21.800
When you find out at 3 o'clock
in the morning from an alert

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.508
that something goes
wrong, a lot of times

00:19:23.508 --> 00:19:25.517
you want to dive into the logs.

00:19:25.517 --> 00:19:27.600
And so one of the things
that we think about a lot

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:31.900
is how do we support that
troubleshooting workflow.

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:34.310
So you might get on
the alert and see

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:38.130
that you have a drop off
in your nGenx connections.

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:40.902
And you're kind of
zoomed into a view.

00:19:40.902 --> 00:19:42.610
When you're looking
at those connections,

00:19:42.610 --> 00:19:45.734
well, when we only have the
monitoring interface, what

00:19:45.734 --> 00:19:47.150
people would do
is they'd say, how

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:50.000
do I double click on that
line and look at the logs?

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:51.920
And that's really what
we're building toward.

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:56.400
And so from here, with a
click, I can click view logs.

00:19:56.400 --> 00:20:00.080
And this will dive you
straight into a filtered view

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:03.550
of the nGenx logs for
the specific resources

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:06.600
in the chart for that
specific period of time.

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:08.900
So you're not
wading through reams

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:11.210
of data trying to
correlate manually

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:12.770
across the different systems.

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:15.550
So we think this is really cool.

00:20:15.550 --> 00:20:17.685
And as we mentioned
in the keynote,

00:20:17.685 --> 00:20:19.310
it's really simple
to go the other way.

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:21.018
So I could search and
filter on the logs,

00:20:21.018 --> 00:20:23.410
and then I could create a
metric that feeds the data back

00:20:23.410 --> 00:20:26.079
into my dashboards, makes
it available for alerting,

00:20:26.079 --> 00:20:26.620
and so forth.

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:33.110
One other point here, we've
talked a lot about alerting.

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:37.190
Let's make sure you
understand how alerting works.

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:40.860
So from this interface, I
could create a learning policy

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:42.100
by going to alerting.

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:43.730
We can also do it
from the charts.

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:46.430
So again, if you're looking
at that zoomed in interface

00:20:46.430 --> 00:20:49.640
for a specific metric, you can
click Create Alerting Policy,

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:52.110
and it will take you directly
to that configuration

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:53.140
for that metric.

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:56.850
Here I'll do a little bit
more of a sophisticated alert.

00:20:56.850 --> 00:21:00.370
So first you could
create basic alerts

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:04.940
where, I don't know if, I want
to look across my GCE instances

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:16.400
in my Apache cluster
on CGP and notify me

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:22.580
if any member of the
cluster has CPU utilization

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:24.390
above a certain level.

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:28.440
This is a very low utilization
cluster as you can see.

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:31.190
We could do that, right?

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:33.700
But the Stackdriver
system, because

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:36.960
of this notion of
alerts, you can

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:39.400
use it to have more
sophisticated alerts.

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:44.580
So for example on that cluster,
if you're running many VMs,

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:48.230
you don't really care often
about a single machine having

00:21:48.230 --> 00:21:49.350
an issue.

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:50.700
I certainly don't.

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:54.310
What I care about is the
aggregate across the cluster.

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:58.640
So here for that GCP cluster,
what I might want to do

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:03.170
is alert on whether that
CPU again-- or let's

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:09.540
say Apache-- requests, the
total number of requests

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:14.360
in the cluster fall
below a certain level.

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:19.860
So here I could say it's a bad
thing if they fall below 160.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:22.090
And then this alert would
go off pretty quickly.

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:29.640
So the alerting feature
set is very powerful.

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:31.800
You're able to sort of
express alerting policies

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:34.630
in a way that ideally
keep us from waking you up

00:22:34.630 --> 00:22:37.170
at 3 o'clock in the morning
when there's not an issue,

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:39.990
but ensure that we do wake you
up at 3 o'clock in the morning

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:42.410
if there is an issue.

00:22:42.410 --> 00:22:45.370
And there are many ways that
we can wake you up at 3 o'clock

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:47.120
in the morning.

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:49.070
The first one is email, right?

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:51.399
Of course I could
generate lots of email.

00:22:51.399 --> 00:22:53.190
Many of you have just
told me, you probably

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:55.100
get a lot of emails on alerts.

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:56.630
We could do that too.

00:22:56.630 --> 00:23:00.110
You could also use Stackdriver
to get SMS messages.

00:23:00.110 --> 00:23:02.470
I suspect you get SMS
messages related to alerts

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:04.860
pretty frequently.

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:08.230
We can drop messages
into Campfire or HipChat

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:13.270
Or if you have a web hook, an
internal application that we

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:15.420
can POST JSON to
over a web hook,

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:17.950
the application can do that.

00:23:17.950 --> 00:23:21.110
But perhaps one of our
most powerful integrations

00:23:21.110 --> 00:23:23.290
and most widely
used integrations

00:23:23.290 --> 00:23:25.240
is the one with PagerDuty.

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:27.580
And so to talk to
us more about that,

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:30.447
I'd like to welcome
David to the stage.

00:23:30.447 --> 00:23:32.030
DAVID HAYES: All
right, thank you Dan.

00:23:32.030 --> 00:23:33.750
Obviously PagerDuty
is super excited

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:36.170
to be partnering with
Google and Stackdriver.

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:37.670
But I'm especially
excited because I

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:40.235
get to show you a lot
of great new features

00:23:40.235 --> 00:23:41.110
with our integration.

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:43.170
So our relationship
with Stackdriver

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.380
has extended back
for many years.

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:47.860
But today we're
unveiling a bucket

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:49.510
of great new functionality.

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:51.430
But first, what is PagerDuty?

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:54.470
What we solve is the
many to many problem--

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:56.630
relationship, sorry,
between your monitoring

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:58.180
tools and your people.

00:23:58.180 --> 00:24:00.820
So you manage a
sophisticated infrastructure

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:02.940
and you monitor that with,
hopefully, Stackdriver.

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:03.990
It's a great tool.

00:24:03.990 --> 00:24:06.730
But you may also be using
Splunk for log management.

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:09.220
You may sometimes find out
about things via tickets.

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:11.600
You may be migrating
off a legacy tool.

00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:13.600
You may have some
custom tools that you've

00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:15.590
implemented for
some edge cases that

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:16.920
are unique to your business.

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:19.382
And what we do is we roll
those up into services.

00:24:19.382 --> 00:24:21.840
Because at the end of the day
or when something goes wrong,

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:23.548
you don't care what
tool got to it first.

00:24:23.548 --> 00:24:25.250
You care what service is broken.

00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:27.430
And then instead of mapping
those services directly

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:29.730
to people, we map
them to the teams.

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:32.665
So you were able to
manage your schedule

00:24:32.665 --> 00:24:34.790
so you know who's on call
for a particular service.

00:24:34.790 --> 00:24:36.209
Maybe I'm on call now.

00:24:36.209 --> 00:24:37.250
You're on call next week.

00:24:37.250 --> 00:24:40.070
We have a third person who's
on call the week after that.

00:24:40.070 --> 00:24:41.920
And then we escalate
between those.

00:24:41.920 --> 00:24:43.985
So if I don't pick
up my alert within,

00:24:43.985 --> 00:24:45.360
let's say, 10
minutes, it's going

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:46.943
to escalate to
somebody else, and then

00:24:46.943 --> 00:24:49.600
possibly to a third layer
to make sure that it's

00:24:49.600 --> 00:24:51.020
resolved within the SLA.

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:53.650
And then finally the
bit that Dan alluded to,

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:55.700
we do wake you up at 3:00 AM.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:57.910
Our goal in life is to
help you fix problems

00:24:57.910 --> 00:25:00.644
as soon as possible,
but we are very noisy.

00:25:00.644 --> 00:25:01.310
We'll phone you.

00:25:01.310 --> 00:25:03.127
We'll push messages to you.

00:25:03.127 --> 00:25:03.710
We'll SMS you.

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:05.660
We'll email you.

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:07.880
So what's exciting and
what's new as of today

00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:09.850
is the Stackdriver-PagerDuty
integration

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:12.140
has three great new checkboxes.

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:13.490
It's bidirectional.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:15.190
So whichever your
single source of truth

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:16.990
is, whatever screen
you're looking at,

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:19.129
whether it's Stackdriver
or whether it's PagerDuty,

00:25:19.129 --> 00:25:21.170
you're going to see exactly
the same information.

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:23.240
So if your team
uses Stackdriver,

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:25.470
my team uses
PagerDuty, we're going

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:27.330
to be on the exact
same page as to what's

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:30.790
broken, what the state of the
world is, and who's on it.

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:33.380
We show the on call
schedules now in Stackdriver.

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:35.490
Again, so at a glance
you're able to see

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:38.340
who's responsible for fixing
anything should it happen.

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:41.330
And my favorite feature,
we now embed live charts

00:25:41.330 --> 00:25:43.810
from Stackdriver in
your PagerDuty alerts.

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:46.450
So you'll be able to dive
in the second we notify you

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:49.550
and start resolving the issue.

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:52.250
Let's see some of
these things in action.

00:25:52.250 --> 00:25:54.910
As Dan pointed out
earlier, we have

00:25:54.910 --> 00:25:57.890
the schedule displayed
as a dashboard

00:25:57.890 --> 00:26:00.060
widget in Stackdriver.

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:01.061
It basically just works.

00:26:01.061 --> 00:26:03.476
And actually if you don't mind,
I'm going to go off script

00:26:03.476 --> 00:26:05.780
and I'm going to thank your
design team, because that

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:07.450
is a beautiful looking widget.

00:26:07.450 --> 00:26:09.270
It really captures
a concise view

00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:10.914
of understanding
who's on call and when

00:26:10.914 --> 00:26:11.872
that's going to change.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:16.350
Stackdriver has a
concept of incidence,

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:18.340
and obviously
PagerDuty does as well.

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:20.430
And now the two
will stay in sync.

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:24.040
So we have here a
Stackdriver incident

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:25.630
referring to something broken.

00:26:25.630 --> 00:26:28.124
And then in PagerDuty, we
have the same incident.

00:26:28.124 --> 00:26:30.540
And as you can see, it links
right back to the Stackdriver

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:34.070
incident so that whatever your
next step's in resolution,

00:26:34.070 --> 00:26:36.480
you're able to get there
quickly and effectively.

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:38.790
And like I said, my
favorite feature,

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:41.210
we show the live
Stackdriver chart.

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:42.990
So this is interactive.

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:45.480
I can zoom in to find out
exactly when and how something

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:46.320
went wrong.

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:49.504
You can tell just by looking at
this that I'm faking my demo,

00:26:49.504 --> 00:26:51.420
that we basically took
a process and we killed

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:52.940
it and we were alerting on it.

00:26:55.460 --> 00:26:57.210
But the big thing
you get from this

00:26:57.210 --> 00:26:58.960
is as soon as you
get your alert,

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:00.710
you're able to tell
what kind of situation

00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:02.800
you're dealing with
before you've even logged

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:05.070
in to any other
tools, before you've

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:07.320
jumped on to whatever
your resolution path is,

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:09.557
because the graph
tells you an awful lot.

00:27:09.557 --> 00:27:12.140
So here's what we're going to
do the technically sophisticated

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:15.230
part of my presentation and I'm
going to gesticulate wildly,

00:27:15.230 --> 00:27:17.420
because there's very
different kinds of graphs.

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:19.687
So if you've got a
threshold set here

00:27:19.687 --> 00:27:21.270
and it's broken by
what we technically

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:24.120
refer to as a chugga,
chugga, chugga gradual thing,

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:25.630
you've got a very
different problem

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:28.130
than if you've got
a metric that's

00:27:28.130 --> 00:27:30.530
chugging along at 1%
to 2% of the threshold,

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:32.590
and then shoots up dramatically.

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:35.150
Similarly, it's
up to the minute.

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:38.880
So let's say it takes those
10, those 15 minutes for you

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:43.260
to get woken up and focus
your eyes on your phone,

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:45.680
you're now looking
at this second's data

00:27:45.680 --> 00:27:48.217
as opposed to when the
incident was triggered.

00:27:48.217 --> 00:27:50.800
So you might even be looking at
the resolution or the incident

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:51.750
might have evolved.

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:53.920
So perhaps it's crested
above the threshold

00:27:53.920 --> 00:27:55.840
and it's trending down,
which is a good sign.

00:27:55.840 --> 00:27:58.220
Or perhaps it went
above the threshold

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:00.650
and it plummeted to zero,
which is a very bad sign

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:02.774
and you know you're going
to be dealing with a very

00:28:02.774 --> 00:28:03.860
different kind of issue.

00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:06.370
And naturally because we've
been working with Stackdriver

00:28:06.370 --> 00:28:09.790
for so long, the
configuration is super easy.

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:11.717
You just drop in
your authentication,

00:28:11.717 --> 00:28:14.050
or if you get to it before I
have a chance to change it,

00:28:14.050 --> 00:28:15.960
you can drop in
my authentication.

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:17.830
And you've got a
perfect-- you've

00:28:17.830 --> 00:28:20.740
got a smooth hand between
Stackdriver and PagerDuty.

00:28:20.740 --> 00:28:23.320
And speaking of
smooth hand-offs, Dan?

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:25.240
DAN BELCHER: Thank
you very much David.

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:29.480
We've been really a great
partner with PagerDuty

00:28:29.480 --> 00:28:30.975
now for several years.

00:28:30.975 --> 00:28:34.290
It's one of our most important
integrations and widely used.

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:36.360
If you want to learn
more about PagerDuty,

00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:38.170
they have a spot in
the partner zone.

00:28:38.170 --> 00:28:42.380
So you could go there to
ask questions, learn more.

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:46.190
Integrations, there are many
in the Stackdriver service.

00:28:46.190 --> 00:28:48.060
Some of the newest
and most exciting ones

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:49.656
are with our big data services.

00:28:49.656 --> 00:28:51.030
And so what I'd
like to do is ask

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:53.610
Vas to demonstrate
how you can use

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:58.750
Google Stackdriver with the big
data services and Google Cloud.

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:00.150
VAS BALA: Thank you Dan.

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:02.970
We worked really
hard to make sure

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:06.050
that Stackdriver delivers an
awesome experience right out

00:29:06.050 --> 00:29:07.220
of the box.

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:10.600
Hopefully you saw that in the
keynote and with Dan's demo

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:11.790
today.

00:29:11.790 --> 00:29:16.460
And we're pretty confident
that for 90% of the use cases

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:18.990
our customers will
have, Stackdriver

00:29:18.990 --> 00:29:20.920
is more than adequate.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:24.790
But what about the
10% of the use cases

00:29:24.790 --> 00:29:30.410
when Stackdriver isn't quite
the answer to your question?

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:34.930
You know today, other
vendors offer products

00:29:34.930 --> 00:29:38.430
where it's either their
product or nothing.

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:40.510
We've taken a very
different position.

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:43.190
We think Stackdriver's
totally awesome.

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:45.940
But if you would like to get
directly at the underlying

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:49.450
data, we're going to give
you an ad hoc interface that

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:52.470
lets you play directly with
the data with Stackdriver

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:53.540
out of the way.

00:29:53.540 --> 00:29:56.080
And I'm going to give you two
demonstrations that show you

00:29:56.080 --> 00:29:58.060
how you can pull this off.

00:29:58.060 --> 00:30:02.359
The first is an
integration with BigQuery.

00:30:02.359 --> 00:30:04.650
As you heard in the keynote,
one of the things BigQuery

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:08.700
is really awesome for is dealing
with petabyte scale data,

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:12.730
with a simple SQL
like interface.

00:30:12.730 --> 00:30:15.550
Now where do you see
petabyte scale data

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:18.706
where you need a SQL
like interface-- logs.

00:30:18.706 --> 00:30:20.830
So I'm going to show you
in the first demonstration

00:30:20.830 --> 00:30:23.960
how, with just a
mouse click or two,

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:27.100
you can stream logs from
Stackdriver to BigQuery

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:28.870
and go into the
BigQuery console,

00:30:28.870 --> 00:30:32.100
and you have a query
playground at your fingertips.

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:34.314
Let's see that demo first.

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:41.220
So what I'm showing you
here is your logs view.

00:30:41.220 --> 00:30:45.230
And you can see these
are actually logs

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:47.050
coming from our Amazon project.

00:30:47.050 --> 00:30:50.060
So we are cross platform.

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:53.500
Now let's say that
the particular way

00:30:53.500 --> 00:30:58.320
that we allow you to query logs
is not quite what you want.

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:01.080
You'll notice here
there's an Export button.

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:04.550
So when you click on
Exports, it takes you

00:31:04.550 --> 00:31:07.740
to this view where you can
pick the service you want.

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:10.650
So I'm picking my
Amazon EC2 service.

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:14.830
And if you already have a
BigQuery instance set up,

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:20.070
you can select it over here, or
you can create a new one right

00:31:20.070 --> 00:31:22.739
here from this menu, and
Stackdriver will take care

00:31:22.739 --> 00:31:24.030
of all of the creation for you.

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:27.680
So there's no configuration
of BigQuery or any such thing.

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:30.860
Essentially two mouse
clicks, select, select,

00:31:30.860 --> 00:31:32.700
you've got a BigQuery instance.

00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:34.840
And now what happens
is really cool.

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:37.720
Stackdriver will now
automatically stream

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:40.160
your log data into BigQuery.

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:42.350
You don't need to
do anything else.

00:31:42.350 --> 00:31:48.550
You can just go straight to
the BigQuery query editor.

00:31:48.550 --> 00:31:51.250
And here I have
a query for which

00:31:51.250 --> 00:31:54.689
I'm going to look at
my Apache access logs.

00:31:54.689 --> 00:31:57.230
And if you're not familiar with
the BigQuery query interface,

00:31:57.230 --> 00:31:58.340
it's pretty cool.

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:04.070
You can see your query, the
table schema details, preview.

00:32:04.070 --> 00:32:06.910
And you can just edit your
custom query and just run

00:32:06.910 --> 00:32:08.430
the query.

00:32:08.430 --> 00:32:11.960
In this particular case,
I've picked a canned query

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:18.380
where I want to count the number
of HTTP 200 and 404 errors.

00:32:18.380 --> 00:32:20.500
The Stackdriver
logs viewer is very

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:23.130
good at searching for
keywords in the logs,

00:32:23.130 --> 00:32:26.870
but it's not set up to count
the number of HTTP 200 and 404

00:32:26.870 --> 00:32:27.370
errors.

00:32:27.370 --> 00:32:32.180
So just to show you an
example, I've taken this case.

00:32:32.180 --> 00:32:33.600
And the query's
pretty long here.

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:36.270
And I'm also going
to group the results

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:38.960
by services and by region.

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:42.080
So let's run that query.

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:44.540
And here are the answers.

00:32:44.540 --> 00:32:47.380
It's grouped by
region, shows you

00:32:47.380 --> 00:32:51.720
how many 200 and
404 errors I get.

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:54.350
So here's a very
simple demonstration

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:59.500
of the power of running on
Stackdriver on the Google Cloud

00:32:59.500 --> 00:33:00.400
Platform.

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:01.600
This is cross platform.

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:04.330
We took all the logs that are
already in Stackdriver driver

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:08.030
and we piped it into BigQuery
and gave you an ad hoc query

00:33:08.030 --> 00:33:10.910
interface to play with.

00:33:10.910 --> 00:33:18.530
All right, so onto the
second demonstration.

00:33:18.530 --> 00:33:21.500
What about your time
series metric data?

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:24.430
That's another
valuable source of data

00:33:24.430 --> 00:33:26.880
that Stackdriver
provides, I think,

00:33:26.880 --> 00:33:29.290
a super cool awesome
interface for.

00:33:29.290 --> 00:33:32.090
But maybe there
are situations when

00:33:32.090 --> 00:33:33.770
you want to slice
and dice the data

00:33:33.770 --> 00:33:35.910
and visualize it in
a way that is not

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:39.420
available in the
Stackdriver dashboard?

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:43.170
And perhaps you want to
run some third party tools

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:46.460
or open source tools on the
time series metric data.

00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:48.290
How would you do that?

00:33:48.290 --> 00:33:50.510
Well, welcome to Datalab.

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:53.760
It was briefly mentioned at the
keynote along with the BigQuery

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:54.700
presentation.

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:57.760
So let me just remind
you, Google Cloud Datalab

00:33:57.760 --> 00:34:00.220
was a product we
announced last year.

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:01.750
It's in beta.

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:06.460
And it is based on Jupyter,
formally called IPython.

00:34:06.460 --> 00:34:09.190
And it is baked into the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:34:09.190 --> 00:34:14.020
So you could start up a Datalab
instance on any GCP project.

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:17.800
And it gives you an
interactive Python environment

00:34:17.800 --> 00:34:18.659
to work with.

00:34:18.659 --> 00:34:21.310
And you can just directly
program away in Python.

00:34:21.310 --> 00:34:24.110
And as you saw in the
keynote, Datalab already

00:34:24.110 --> 00:34:29.980
supports large data services on
GCP such as BigQuery, Cloud SQL

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:30.820
et cetera.

00:34:30.820 --> 00:34:32.409
And what we're going
to show you now

00:34:32.409 --> 00:34:36.260
is we've also added support
to tap into your time series

00:34:36.260 --> 00:34:39.100
metric data inside Stackdriver.

00:34:39.100 --> 00:34:42.210
And I think it's super cool.

00:34:42.210 --> 00:34:44.670
Let's take a look at that.

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:46.690
So here we go.

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:50.570
What we're looking at here
is a Datalab instance.

00:34:50.570 --> 00:34:54.080
And I'm pointing to this demo
project on which Dan showed

00:34:54.080 --> 00:34:56.929
all of his graphs earlier.

00:34:56.929 --> 00:35:01.180
And this is an IPython notebook.

00:35:01.180 --> 00:35:06.150
So everything you see in
here consists of Python cells

00:35:06.150 --> 00:35:07.590
that are all executable.

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:09.160
And when you execute
a Python cell,

00:35:09.160 --> 00:35:11.697
its results are shown
immediately beneath it.

00:35:11.697 --> 00:35:13.530
And it's completely
interactive as I'm going

00:35:13.530 --> 00:35:15.390
to show you in just a second.

00:35:15.390 --> 00:35:18.240
But the other really neat
thing about a notebook

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:22.330
is you can treat it like
a doc, like a document.

00:35:22.330 --> 00:35:25.320
And this notebook can be
shared among other developers

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:26.470
within your team.

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:30.080
So it's awesome for running
what if experiments with data.

00:35:30.080 --> 00:35:31.720
Hey, here's a query.

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:33.280
I ran a time series metric.

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:34.220
Look at what it found.

00:35:34.220 --> 00:35:36.440
And just share it with
your other developers.

00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:39.900
It's super awesome for
those kinds of tasks.

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:42.500
So let's first go
through a few canned

00:35:42.500 --> 00:35:45.250
exampled I've got in here
just to explain how this works

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:46.880
and get a feel for it.

00:35:46.880 --> 00:35:49.980
So what this shows
is at the very top

00:35:49.980 --> 00:35:52.320
here I've got my import.

00:35:52.320 --> 00:35:55.450
So from Stackdriver
import monitoring as GCM.

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:59.270
So this variable
GCM, that object GCM

00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:03.950
contains all of the utilities
that we built into Datalab.

00:36:03.950 --> 00:36:06.750
What's happening here
is I'm running a query.

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:08.750
This is just querying
the time series

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:12.530
metric data inside Stackdriver
for a metric called CPU

00:36:12.530 --> 00:36:15.170
utilization over
the last two hours.

00:36:15.170 --> 00:36:19.240
And I am restricting
the result set

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:22.690
to only be the data
for instances that

00:36:22.690 --> 00:36:25.660
have a prefix called front end.

00:36:25.660 --> 00:36:28.420
And in addition,
the dotted line says

00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:32.270
I want to downsample the data
by five minutes using mean

00:36:32.270 --> 00:36:35.620
as my aggregation function.

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:40.360
And the results go into this
Python object called q1.

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:42.690
Now in the Python
object, we've already

00:36:42.690 --> 00:36:45.930
built in a few utilities
like line chart, right?

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:48.480
And this is just a fully
interactive line chart,

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:52.660
just like you saw
with Stackdriver.

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:55.350
We built yet another
utility called heatmap.

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:57.870
Again, the same data
rendered in a different way.

00:36:57.870 --> 00:37:01.310
Sometimes heatmaps are really
useful in understanding

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:05.150
aggregate effects, especially
when your very large clusters,

00:37:05.150 --> 00:37:07.530
line charts tend to
get very, very crowded.

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:10.070
But heatmaps are just
the perfect thing.

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:12.340
Discrepancies will
just pop out at you.

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:17.550
This is a tiny project so you're
not seeing as many instances

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:19.550
on the y-axis here.

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:25.730
But this is not about built in
utilities that Google provides,

00:37:25.730 --> 00:37:28.780
it's about giving you
access to the raw data.

00:37:28.780 --> 00:37:32.420
And this is where the
fun really begins.

00:37:32.420 --> 00:37:34.880
With the same object
q1, you can get the data

00:37:34.880 --> 00:37:37.670
as a pandas DataFrame.

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:39.860
Again, if you recall
from the keynote,

00:37:39.860 --> 00:37:43.530
Google has built in
support for pandas.

00:37:43.530 --> 00:37:50.260
pandas is a widely used open
source statistical analysis

00:37:50.260 --> 00:37:51.570
package.

00:37:51.570 --> 00:37:55.220
It's got a very rich
ecosystem of visualization

00:37:55.220 --> 00:37:57.500
of other libraries
like scikit-learn

00:37:57.500 --> 00:38:00.270
that you can do statistical
machine learning about data.

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:02.880
You can really
play with that data

00:38:02.880 --> 00:38:05.690
in just a super cool
awesome way with pandas.

00:38:05.690 --> 00:38:09.500
And what we show in here is
I just pull that data out

00:38:09.500 --> 00:38:10.410
as a DataFrame.

00:38:10.410 --> 00:38:12.150
This is a pandas object.

00:38:12.150 --> 00:38:16.790
So this is a pandas utility that
says just show the first five

00:38:16.790 --> 00:38:18.630
rows of the DataFrame.

00:38:18.630 --> 00:38:20.320
And you can see the
data right there.

00:38:20.320 --> 00:38:21.970
You've got the metric data.

00:38:21.970 --> 00:38:24.190
First column are timestamps.

00:38:24.190 --> 00:38:27.600
You can see there's a
hierarchy of column headers

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:32.480
that naturally tell you what
the levels of the hierarchy are.

00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:36.886
You've got a project,
zone, group, et cetera.

00:38:36.886 --> 00:38:39.260
And then you can do lots of
cool things in the DataFrame.

00:38:39.260 --> 00:38:41.844
Now I don't have the time to get
into all the different things

00:38:41.844 --> 00:38:43.468
you can do, but I
just want to give you

00:38:43.468 --> 00:38:45.700
a flavor of the kind of data
that you can manipulate.

00:38:45.700 --> 00:38:47.380
Over here I'm just
applying a filter

00:38:47.380 --> 00:38:51.030
which takes a regular expression
on all the column headers.

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:55.560
And it just finds any column
that matches europe-west1-b.

00:38:55.560 --> 00:38:58.640
And it's showing me
the data for that.

00:38:58.640 --> 00:39:01.290
I can aggregate data
by project ID and zone

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:03.590
by just giving the
different levels,

00:39:03.590 --> 00:39:06.120
by using mean as my
aggregation function,

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:08.590
and then doing a GROUP BY.

00:39:08.590 --> 00:39:12.800
And I'm just showing the first
five rows of that DataFrame,

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:14.870
and so on and so forth.

00:39:14.870 --> 00:39:18.430
So before I wind
up, when we were

00:39:18.430 --> 00:39:21.790
doing the demo or
the keynote, right

00:39:21.790 --> 00:39:24.310
in the middle of the demo,
Dan was standing over

00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:26.540
in the corner, and
he toggled the load

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:31.100
so that the load shifted from
AWS resources to GCP resources.

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:34.400
And just out of curiosity,
I decided to hack this up

00:39:34.400 --> 00:39:38.680
before I got here to see whether
we can actually see the Apache

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:42.010
request count metric
for the last two hours--

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:44.050
and I'm doing a downsampling
by five minutes--

00:39:44.050 --> 00:39:47.340
if I can actually see that
load shift as a heat map.

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:53.350
So I have no idea what I'm going
to see, but let's check it out.

00:39:53.350 --> 00:39:57.220
Oh, I just caught the
load shift right here.

00:39:57.220 --> 00:39:58.890
I could have widened
the hours to three

00:39:58.890 --> 00:40:00.520
and you would have seen this.

00:40:00.520 --> 00:40:03.700
But what this is showing
is the top two instances

00:40:03.700 --> 00:40:06.140
apparently were not involved
in the load toggling.

00:40:06.140 --> 00:40:08.790
I think they were
backup instances.

00:40:08.790 --> 00:40:12.300
But then you see
earlier in the time

00:40:12.300 --> 00:40:15.740
there was a shift from
load from the AWS resources

00:40:15.740 --> 00:40:17.250
to the GCP resources.

00:40:17.250 --> 00:40:20.590
OK, so to recap,
what we're showing

00:40:20.590 --> 00:40:24.080
you here is a way to directly
tap into the underlying

00:40:24.080 --> 00:40:28.530
data in Stackdriver with an ad
hoc interface in the situation

00:40:28.530 --> 00:40:31.770
where the super awesome
Stackdriver interface is not

00:40:31.770 --> 00:40:32.520
adequate.

00:40:32.520 --> 00:40:35.380
With that, let hand the
control back to Dan.

00:40:35.380 --> 00:40:37.550
DAN BELCHER: Thank
you very much Vas.

00:40:37.550 --> 00:40:40.310
All right, so to wrap
it up, first of all

00:40:40.310 --> 00:40:43.110
we want to thank
you for your time.

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:46.270
It was a pleasure to tell
you about the new Google

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:48.440
Stackdriver.

00:40:48.440 --> 00:40:50.280
We hit on a few key themes.

00:40:50.280 --> 00:40:52.630
So the first thing
I'll recap is we

00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:54.300
try to make it
really easy for you

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:55.970
to get the visibility
that you need,

00:40:55.970 --> 00:40:58.780
whether it's at the Cloud
Platform layer, the system

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:00.990
layer, or the application layer.

00:41:00.990 --> 00:41:02.630
Next we talked
about integrations,

00:41:02.630 --> 00:41:06.370
be it the monitoring
integration with our agent,

00:41:06.370 --> 00:41:10.000
our logging agent Fluentd,
or third party notification

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:13.490
tools like PagerDuty and Slack
and Campfire and HipChat,

00:41:13.490 --> 00:41:17.430
and also these new and exciting
integrations with other tools

00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:19.320
within the Google
Cloud Platform.

00:41:19.320 --> 00:41:23.300
The BigQuery logging integration
was launched last year

00:41:23.300 --> 00:41:24.180
to great applause.

00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:26.860
Many of you are
using it actively.

00:41:26.860 --> 00:41:29.805
The Datalab integration will be
available for you very shortly

00:41:29.805 --> 00:41:33.610
and we're really excited about
that on the time series front.

00:41:33.610 --> 00:41:34.720
We talked about insights.

00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:37.050
Now what we think about
and sort of obsess

00:41:37.050 --> 00:41:40.950
over is, how can we support
an operator or a developer

00:41:40.950 --> 00:41:43.910
on call going from
that PagerDuty alert

00:41:43.910 --> 00:41:46.980
or that SMS to go all
the way through whatever

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:49.260
signals they need to get
through to get to root cause.

00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:51.520
And we show the
initial stages of that

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:54.410
in terms of integrating,
monitoring, and logging.

00:41:54.410 --> 00:41:57.700
You could also see integration
between monitoring and tracing

00:41:57.700 --> 00:41:58.920
and error reporting.

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:01.550
And so we intend to
go further and further

00:42:01.550 --> 00:42:05.290
in terms of bringing these
tools and capabilities together.

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:08.870
And then finally we talked
about Cloud Platform

00:42:08.870 --> 00:42:12.700
and how we embrace
Google Cloud, AWS.

00:42:12.700 --> 00:42:16.230
And we intend to support
additional platforms as you

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:18.820
tell us that you want us to.

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:21.590
And so thank you very
much for your time.

00:42:21.590 --> 00:42:25.381
You can try Google Stackdriver
free in beta right now.

00:42:25.381 --> 00:42:26.880
And if you have any
questions, we'll

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:29.700
be at the infrastructure
and operations booth

00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:30.610
out on the floor.

00:42:30.610 --> 00:42:32.390
Thank you.

